Every other command reads ${he}, so this is the one to run first. It never
overwrites an existing file: edit that one, against the field reference at ${Nn}`).action(lO),ja(e.command("search").description("Read messages: match locale text, the keypath, or both at once. Queries are regular expressions").argument("[query...]","Pattern the text must match (case-insensitive). Several are searched in one call").option("--key <pattern>","Keypath to match, narrowing the text query rather than replacing it. `-` reads exact keypaths from stdin, one per line").option("--locale <locale>","Search and show only this locale").option("--json","Print the whole call as one JSON document, for joining against something else").option("--limit <n>","Max matches to print in full, per term. Uncapped unless you say so")).addHelpText("after",`
Every query is a case-insensitive regular expression, over the text by default:

  loccy-tool search Reservierung --locale de
  loccy-tool search Reservierung Buchung Termin --locale de
  loccy-tool search 'Could ?n.t' --locale en

--key matches the keypath instead, for the other question: which message is this key from
source, or what sits under this group. Alone it is the whole search; with a term it narrows it.

  loccy-tool search --key login
  loccy-tool search --key '^Tickets\\..*Tooltip$'
  loccy-tool search '\\.$' --key '^(?!.*[Tt]ooltip)' --locale en

A phrase out of the UI is the same thing with its pattern characters escaped:

  loccy-tool search "Are you sure\\? \\(optional\\)"

Keep patterns simple: nested quantifiers can hang the search.

Holding a key list already, from a grep over the source that calls them, pipe it in and read
the values back rather than opening the locale files. Those are matched exactly, never as
patterns:

  grep -rhoE "t\\('[A-Za-z0-9_.]+'\\)" src | sed -E "s/.*'(.*)'.*/\\1/" | sort -u \\
    | loccy-tool search --key - --locale en --json

--json prints one document per call: every block, its total, and each match with the value
each locale holds. That is the form to join against grep output or feed to jq.

Each match prints its value in every locale. Several terms get a block each, in the order
given, which is how a glossary candidate is checked for drift in one call.

A namespace goes in --ns, and a keypath is always bare.`).action(dO),ja(e.command("upsert-message").description("Add or update keys across locale files, multiple locales in one call").option("--styleguided <token>","The token standing for this project's writing rules")).addHelpText("after",`
JSON on stdin, keyed by keypath. Cheapest is one call carrying every key and locale you are
changing:

  loccy-tool upsert-message <<'EOF'
  {"login.title":{"en":"Sign in","de":"Anmelden"},"login.ok":{"en":"Continue","de":"Weiter"}}
  EOF

Only the locales you pass are written. "" deletes the key
from that locale:

  {"login.ok":{"en":"Success","en-US":""}}

`).action(wO),ja(e.command("remove-message").description("Remove keys from every locale file that holds them").argument("<key...>","Keypaths to remove, e.g. login.title.")).addHelpText("after",`
  loccy-tool remove-message login.old                  # i18n setup without namespaces
  loccy-tool remove-message login.old --ns auth        # namespaced i18n setup
  loccy-tool remove-message login.old signup.old       # several at once, all or nothing`).action(OO),ja(e.command("rename-key").description("Rename keys across every locale file")).addHelpText("after",`
Renames go in as JSON on stdin, one pair or many, applied all or nothing:

  loccy-tool rename-key <<'EOF'
  {"login.title":"login.heading","login.ok":"login.confirm"}
  EOF

A namespace goes in --ns, never spelled into the keys, and a rename never crosses one.
A key already in use is never renamed onto: merge deliberately with upsert-message, then remove-message.

Linked references between messages (\`@:old.key\`) follow the rename.`).action(FO),e.command("styleguide-example",{hidden:!0}).action(NO),w4(e),e}gh(process.argv);var yR=process.argv[2],O4=["-h","--help","help"].includes(yR??"")&&process.argv.length===3;(!yR||O4)&&(console.log(Is()),process.exit(0));_R().parseAsync(process.argv).catch(e=>{console.error(`error: ${e instanceof Error?e.message:String(e)}`),process.exit(1)});
